<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

    <worksheet margin="1in" bottom="1.25cm" xml:id="integration-cylindrical-complete">
    <title>Integration in Polar/Cylindrical Coordinates (Complete)</title>
        <page>
        
            <exercise workspace="2in">
            <title>Motivation</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        We've seen how integrating in different coordinates requires consideration of the Jacobian of the
                        transformation. We'll look at this in the specific coordinate systems we've introduce so far in 
                        this course.
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>

            <exercise workspace="6in">
            <title>Jacobian for Polar Coordinates</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                     We analyzed areas in polar coordinates to see that <m>dA = r dr d\theta</m>. Let's verify this expression
                     using the Jacobian.
                    </p>
                </statement>
                <solution>
                    <p>
                        Using <m>x=r\cos(\theta)</m>, <m>y=r\sin(\theta)</m> compute the expression
                        <md>
                            x_r y_{\theta} - x_{\theta} y_r = r\cos^2(\theta) + r\sin^2(\theta) = r
                        </md>.

                        
                    </p>
                </solution>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
            <exercise>
                <title>Examples: Jacobian for Polar Coordinates</title>
                <task workspace="3in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \iint_R x + y \quad dA</m>, where <m>R</m> is the circle of radius <m>2</m> centered at the origin.
                        </p>
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            <m>\displaystyle \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^2 (r \cos(\theta) + r\sin(\theta)) r \quad dr d\theta</m>
                        </p>
                         <p>
                            See the <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/n5yqu1wq3k">integration in Polar coordinates Desmos visual</url> for
                            how to compute these integrals using Desmos.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
                <task workspace="6in">
                    <statement>
                        <p>
                           Verify <m>\displaystyle \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-x^2} \quad dx = \sqrt{\pi}</m>.
                        </p>
                       
                    </statement>
                    <solution>
                        <p>
                            Assuming we know this integral converges, we can write
                            <md>
                                \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-x^2} \quad dx = I
                            </md>.
                            Then 
                            <md>
                                \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-x^2} \quad dx \cdot \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-y^2} \quad dy =  I^2 
                            </md>, and
                            <md>
                                <mrow>  \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-x^2} \quad dx \cdot \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-y^2} \quad dy \amp=  \int_{-\infty}^\infty  \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-x^2} \cdot e^{-y^2} \quad dxdy </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp=\int_{-\infty}^\infty  \int_{-\infty}^\infty e^{-x^2 - y^2}  \quad dxdy </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \int_0^{2\pi} \int_0^\infty e^{-r^2} r \quad dr d\theta </mrow>
                                <mrow> \amp= \pi </mrow>
                            </md>,
                            where the last equality is obtained using the substitution <m>u=r^2</m>, <m>du = 2r dr</m>. 

                            
                            
                            
                        </p>
                        <p>
                            Thus <m>I^2 = \pi</m>, so <m>I = \sqrt{\pi}</m>.
                        </p>
                    </solution>
                </task>
            </exercise>
</page>
<page>
        <exercise workspace="3in">
        <title>Jacobian for Cylindrical Coordinates</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                Recall cylindrical coordinates result from the transformation <m>x=r\cos(\theta)</m>, <m>y= r\sin(\theta)</m>, <m>z=z</m>.
                So the Jacobian for this transformation is
            </p>
            <p>
              <m>\displaystyle J = x_r(y_{\theta} z_z - y_z z_{\theta}) - x_{\theta}(y_r z_z - y_z z_r) + x_z(y_r z_{\theta} -y_{\theta}z_r) </m>
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                which simplifies to <m>r</m>. So <m>dV = r dV'</m>.
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>

        <exercise workspace="5in">
        <title>Integrals Over Surfaces</title>
        <statement>
            <p>
                We'll practice setting up integrals over surfaces of the form
            </p>
        </statement>
        <solution>
            <p>
                <m>S = \{(x,y,z): (x,y) \in D, f(x,y) \leq z \leq g(x,y) \}</m>. The basic form for these integrals is then
                <md>
                    \int \int \int_f^g F(r,\theta,z) r \quad dz dr d\theta
                </md>.
                
            </p>
        </solution>
            
        </exercise>
    
    </page>
    <page>

            <exercise workspace="2in">
                <title>Example: Integrals Over Surfaces</title>
                <statement>
                    <p>
                        Compute <m>\iiint_S 1 \quad dV</m>, where <m>S</m> is the surface below 
                        <m>z=6-x</m>, above <m>z = -\sqrt{4x^2 +4y^2}</m>, and inside <m>x^2 + y^2=3</m> with <m>x \leq 0</m>.
                        
                    </p>
                </statement>
              <solution>
                <p>
                The condition <m>z = 6-x</m> becomes <m>z = 6-r\cos(\theta)</m>, and <m>z = -\sqrt{4x^2 +4y^2}</m> becomes 
                <m>z = -2r</m>. Thus we can compute 
                <md>
                    \int_{\pi/2}^{3\pi/2} \int_0^{\sqrt{3}} \int_{-2r}^{6-r\cos(\theta)} r \quad dz dr d\theta \approx 42.62
                </md>.
                Geometrically, this is the volume of the surface <m>S</m>. See the 
                <url href="https://www.desmos.com/3d/ctrtofnlzb">integration in Cylindrical coordinates Desmos visual</url>.
                
                </p>
              </solution>
                
            </exercise>

    </page>

   

       
    </worksheet>
